{
 "what_you_observe": [
  "Tuesday, February 2. Seven pm.",
  "Phil said in the lobby: Rita, Larry—see you at the party. I'm heading to Pittsburgh.",
  "Rita records Alma at the Rialto and puts her recording on the patch.",
  "Larry said in the lobby: I don't turn back to the lobby. But I say, low, to the nearest one of them, the one who's still holding a saucer: 'Rialto's closing. I'm going to get a shot before the lights go out. Be back for the party.' It's not a question. I'm already walking.",
  "Larry records Alma on his last charged take. The reel stays with him.",
  "You are now at Rialto.",
  "There is time for one evening visit. The ballroom lights are on and the station line is open."
 ],
 "your_own_memories": [
  {
   "what_you_did": "Rita records Alma at the Rialto and puts her recording on the patch.",
   "what_you_said": "Alma, your daughter wants your voice. Talk to her.",
   "your_intention": "Get Alma's voice onto tape before the Rialto closes and the last reel ships out of town."
  }
 ],
 "who_you_are_with": "your present location",
 "physical_choices": {
  "transmit_interview": "Take the regional line at the station and send any usable interview with Alma to her daughter.",
  "go_dancing": "Spend the evening at the ballroom, open to dancing with a colleague.",
  "go_to_party_for_work": "Cover the party with your notebook, without dancing.",
  "take_evening_off": "Choose a quiet evening on your own."
 }
}

{
 "action": "transmit_interview",
 "detail": "I leave the projection booth before the echo of Alma's last sentence finishes. My shoes are still damp from the lobby carpet. I walk past the ballroom without slowing—the waltz is already starting, someone is laughing, a glass clinks. I don't turn. I go to the station line, the one with the cracked faceplate and the dial that sticks on the P. I lift the receiver. The dial hums under my thumb. I dial Pittsburgh. The operator's voice, small and bored, asks for the party. I say: regional line, Rialto station, one recording, personal, for a family in Pittsburgh. She asks for the number. I give it. I don't remember where I wrote it down. I do remember the number. She patches it through. I thread the tape—the one with Alma's voice, the one with the forty-two winters in her throat—into the transmitter. I press the feed button. I sit on the stool. I listen. Alma's voice goes out into the wire, flat and a little thin, the way it sounds when it's not filling a room. I listen to all of it. When it ends I let the tape run out, let the reel spin free. I say to the operator: thank you, that's all. I set the receiver down. I don't wipe my hands. I stand up. The ballroom is still going. I walk out the side door into the February air, and I don't go back in. I don't go to the party. I go to the parking lot, sit in the car with the engine off, and I stay there a while, breathing, listening to the waltz leak through the brick.","intention": "Alma's voice reaches her daughter in Pittsburgh tonight, on the wire, before the reel ships and the line goes quiet and the evening belongs to everyone else.","words": "To the operator, flat and quick: regional line, one recording, personal, family in Pittsburgh. To no one else. I do not call Alma back. I do not say I'm sending it. I just do it. And when I walk out the side door, the waltz is louder on the other side of the glass, and I don't look back."
}
